  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nDry and seasonably cold weather will prevail through at least \nearly Wednesday across central and southeast IL. A complex \nstorm system looks to impact Illinois from late Wed into Thu \nnight, and precipitation could be mixed including possible snow\naccumulations Wed night into Thu evening for Thanksgiving\nholiday.\n\nThe 09Z/3 am surface analysis shows 1032-1034 mb Canadian high \npressure over central ND and ridging southward across the Great\nPlains. Meanwhile the cold front that swept through IL on Sunday\nhad pushed into southeast KY and mid TN, with nw winds ushering\nin much colder air into the Prairie State. Temperatures were in\nthe lower 30s over much of central IL at 3 am, with mid 30s \nsoutheast of I-70 in southeast IL. (Galesburg was down to 28F \nand NWS Lincoln at 29F). The Nighttime Microphysics imagery \nshows clear skies across IL.\n\nCanadian high pressure to strengthen to 1035-1038 mb as it\nsettles into sw MN by evening and into the upper MS river valley\nby dawn Tue, and sprawling across the Great Lakes region by\nsunset Tue. This should ensure dry weather across the area\nthrough at least early Wed while bringing seasonably cold\ntemperatures. Scattered cu/stratocumulus clouds to appear over \nlater this morning and afternoon especially over the IL river \nvalley. Much cooler highs today in the low to mid 40s in central\nIL and upper 40s south of I-70. NW winds gusting up to around \n20 mph at times to add a chill to the air today. Winds to \ndiminish some after sunset this evening veering northerly \ntonight and NE Tue into Wed. Colder tonight with lows in the \nlower 20s over much of CWA and may even be a few spots in the \nupper teens. More clouds will be noted over west central and sw \nIL tonight but mostly clear skies overall for CWA. Clouds \nincrease during Tue with mostly cloudy skies by mid Tue \nafternoon. Highs in the lower 40s Tue in central IL and mid 40s \nsoutheast IL. Galesburg may struggle to reach 40F. Lows in the \nmid 20s Tue night in central IL and upper 20s southeast IL with \nmore cloud cover expected. Stays chilly Wed and Thu with highs \nmostly in the lower 40s and subfreezing lows Wed and Thu night \nin the upper 20s/lower 30s.\n\nA double barrel upper level system evolves Tue night and Wed\nover the Central Plains and lower MS river valley. An inverted\nsurface trof extends northward from surface low off LA delta coast\ninto the mid MS river valley Wed and starting to pump moisture\nnorthward into southeast IL by mid day Wed and as far north/nw\nas the IL river by 6 pm Wed. Initially pcpn type should mainly\nbe light rain and possible mixed with light snow but little or\nno accumulations on Wed. Cutoff upper level low moves over\nOzarks of southern MO and northern AR Wed night and ENE over \nthe lower Ohio river valley on Thu with coastal low lifting up\nthe East Coast Thu and Thu night. Frontogenetic forcing \nincreasing over IL and increasing precipitation chances and \namounts Wed night and Thu. Snow chances also increase during \nWed night into Thu morning especially west of I-57. Still \ndifferences with the forecast models as Ecmwf is showing more \nrain over IL while GFS showing some snow accumulations. NBM has\n20-40% chance of 1 inch or more of wet snow west of I-57 and \neast of the IL river Wed night into Thu evening. Holiday \ntravelers should continue to monitor later forecasts especially \nif traveling from central IL and to the northeast over New \nEngland. \n\nThe large storms system to eject ne of central IL later Thu\nnight and Friday with mixed pcpn chances diminishing from sw to\nne during Thu night and generally dry Friday through Sunday,\nthough an upper level trof into IL on Saturday could bring small\nchances of showers especially north of I-74. Temperatures to \nmodify back a bit above normal during this weekend with highs \nin the upper 40s to mid 50s, mildest over southern/sw CWA.\n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Nov\n30-Dec 6th has a 60-70% chance of above normal temperatures \nover central and southeast IL, with a 40-50% chance of above \nnormal precipitation.",
